---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro"
import HeroMedia from "../../components/HeroMedia.astro"
import SectionHeader from "../../components/SectionHeader.astro"
import FullWidthText from "../../components/FullWidthText.astro"
import TextImageRow from "../../components/TextImageRow.astro"
import MediaGrid from "../../components/MediaGrid.astro"
import PageTitle from "../../components/PageTitle.astro"
---

<Layout>
    <div class="content">


        <PageTitle 
         title="Helix - Custom CPU Based Render Engine"
         />

        <HeroMedia
                src="/media/projects/helix/renders/hero_render.png"
            alt="Helix Render"
            type="image"
        />

        <FullWidthText>
            <p>
                <br>
                Helix is a CPU rendering software I started briefly in summer, and since late September 2025, have
                dedicated a lot more time to. There were several reasons as to why I wanted to take on this
                project, I was originally inspired by Sebastian Lague's "Coding Adventure" series, especially
                his raytracer and fluid solver projects.
                <br><br>

                This project also opened up a variety of learning opportunities and new challenges for me. At the time of starting this
                project, I had recently gotten a new laptop, and setup Arch Linux for the first time. As well as this, I also started
                using Neovim, a text editor with a lot of keyboard-centric flexibility and a steep learning curve. This
                project gave me the perfect excuse to sink hours into learning, as well as getting comfortable with all of
                these new concepts.
                <br><br>

                Now one of the first questions I get when I talk about my render engine is 'Why call it "Helix?"'.
                Whenever I imagine the word helix, the first thing that comes to mind is DNA, or a "double helix".
                This project was my first time using C++, I wanted to challenge myself, and had the drive to create
                this project, so having to learn a new programming language wasn't going to stop me. One of the first
                questions I had when first learning C++ was, "What's a double?", turns out it's a float with double the
                precision, and that's where the idea first came from to call it Helix, and it's stuck ever since.
                <br><br>

                Below is what Helix can do, as well as the journey that got me here.
                <br><br>
            </p>    
        </FullWidthText>

         <SectionHeader
            title="Supported Data Passes"
            subtitle="Cryptomatte Object and Material, Depth, World Space Position, Normals and Camera Facing Ratio."
            />

 
        <MediaGrid
            items={[
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/data_pass_cryptomatte.png",
                caption: "Cryptomatte"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/data_pass_depth.png",
                caption: "Depth"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/data_pass_world_space_position.png",
                caption: "World Space Position"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/data_pass_normals.png",
                caption: "Normals"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/data_pass_facing_ratio.png",
                caption: "Camera Facing Ratio"
                },
            ]}
        />


        <SectionHeader
            title="Depth of Field"
            subtitle="Using a thin-lens camera model, default settings for the Black Magic Mini Ursa 4.6K."
            />
        
        
        <MediaGrid
                items={[
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/two_dragons_fov_30_2025_oct_27th_10_38.png"
                }
                ]}
            />


        <SectionHeader
            title="Material Types"
            subtitle="Lambertian, Metallic, Dielectric and Emissive"
        />


        <MediaGrid
            items={[
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/purple_dragon.png",
                caption: "Dielectric"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/lambert_two_dragons_400_primary_2025_oct_27th_10_37.png",
                caption: "Lambertian (Matte)"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/metallic_two_dragons_400_primary_2025_oct_27th_11_06.png",
                caption: "Metallic"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/projects/helix/renders/sphere_light_2025_oct_27th_16_01.png",
                caption: "Emissive"
                },
                ]}
            />

            <SectionHeader
                title="The Journey",
                subtitle="An in-depth breakdown of the project from the start to it's current state."
                />


        <TextImageRow
            src="/media/projects/helix/renders/antialiasing_2025_sept_30th_12_08.png"
            alt="A very normal Sphere."
            >
            <p>
        I started my render engine off by initially following along a series called "Raytracing in one weekend." 
        I ran through the initial few chapters of that series. This started with creating very basic images, and 
        exporting through a format called .PPM. After that was adding a basic sphere into the scene, not as geometry,
        but as the mathematical representation. Once that was working, I added basic anti-aliasing and a way to represent what
        would be the normals of the sphere, leading to the image you see here.
            </p>
        </TextImageRow>


        <MediaGrid
            items={[
                {
                src: "/media/test_02.jpg",
                caption: "Cryptomatte"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/test_02.jpg",
                caption: "Depth"
                },
                {
                src: "/media/test_02.jpg",
                caption: "Normals"
                }
            ]}
        />


		<p>This is an example paragraph on the render engine page.</p>
		<a href="/projects/RenderEngine">This points to the page you're currently on!</a>
	</div>
</Layout>
